WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:08.948
One login screen. Many companies behind it, each seeing only its own data.

2
00:00:08.948 --> 00:00:17.183
A software-as-a-service system is an application the provider runs and many customers use, without operating the servers themselves.

3
00:00:17.183 --> 00:00:26.790
Multi-tenancy is the architectural consequence — and it comes with a list of obligations that no platform will meet for you.

4
00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:29.703
NIST states the customer-facing bar.

5
00:00:29.703 --> 00:00:41.267
The application is reached through a browser or a program interface; the customer manages nothing but limited user-specific configuration; the five cloud properties still apply.

6
00:00:41.267 --> 00:00:45.893
A tenant is one customer organization inside that shared product.

7
00:00:45.893 --> 00:00:58.844
A separate copy of the product, installed per customer, can run in a cloud and still fail this test — Amazon’s SaaS guidance calls that a managed service.

8
00:00:58.844 --> 00:01:05.783
SaaS has one onboarding path, one identity experience, and one operational life for every tenant.

9
00:01:06.383 --> 00:01:10.709
The system divides into two planes, and both are required.

10
00:01:10.709 --> 00:01:24.120
The control plane is how the provider runs the tenants as one business: it onboards a tenant, holds the tenant record, authenticates users into a tenant, applies a tier, meters consumption.

11
00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:31.475
If the control plane is missing, each tenant has become a private install, whatever the marketing says.

12
00:01:31.475 --> 00:01:43.588
The application plane is the product the tenant uses, and it is multi-tenant: every request carries a tenant context, and every read and write is constrained by it.

13
00:01:43.588 --> 00:01:50.510
The packaging — monolith or services — is a separate decision. The tenant context is not.

14
00:01:51.110 --> 00:02:02.795
The AWS Well-Architected SaaS Lens groups the concerns, and the chapter turns them into obligations. A tenant identity distinct from a user.

15
00:02:02.795 --> 00:02:12.886
Tenant context on every request, message, log line, and trace. Isolation — a security property and a performance property.

16
00:02:12.886 --> 00:02:26.164
An explicit placement model: silo gives a tenant dedicated resources; pool shares them and enforces the tenant in code and data; bridge mixes the two.

17
00:02:26.164 --> 00:02:35.724
Data partitioning that matches the model, so one tenant can be backed up, restored, or deleted. Automated onboarding.

18
00:02:35.724 --> 00:02:47.409
Tiers, as product and placement decisions. Noisy-neighbor control — quotas and rate limits, the bulkhead at tenant grain. Measured use. Tenant-aware operations.

19
00:02:47.409 --> 00:02:52.720
Limited configuration, not a fork. And a way to leave.

20
00:02:53.320 --> 00:03:02.124
SaaS does not require microservices; a single application can meet every obligation. Microservices change where the requirements have to be repeated.

21
00:03:02.124 --> 00:03:09.252
The access token now carries the tenant, believed from the control plane rather than from the client.

22
00:03:09.252 --> 00:03:13.444
Every message on the broker needs the same tenant context.

23
00:03:13.444 --> 00:03:26.441
Database per service still holds, and inside each schema the tenant model is a second cut — one service pooled, another siloed: the bridge model, legal and easy to get wrong.

24
00:03:26.441 --> 00:03:33.988
The control plane behaves like the platform team: onboarding, tiers, and metering are not rebuilt in every context.

25
00:03:33.988 --> 00:03:39.438
And the aggregate has to check the tenant as well as the rule.

26
00:03:39.438 --> 00:03:52.435
An aggregate that checks ‘may this delivery be cancelled?’ and never checks ‘does this delivery belong to the tenant on the token?’ has enforced the domain and missed the SaaS requirement.

27
00:03:53.035 --> 00:03:56.561
Kubernetes and the cloud sit under both planes.

28
00:03:56.561 --> 00:04:03.614
They can silo a tenant by namespace, cluster, or account, and pool tenants on one Deployment.

29
00:04:03.614 --> 00:04:10.667
They do not put the tenant on the request, and they do not partition the rows.

30
00:04:10.667 --> 00:04:17.720
A network policy can separate siloed namespaces. It cannot separate two tenants that share a table.

31
00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:25.957
A generated service that stores orders and exposes an API can look complete and still be a single-tenant program.

32
00:04:25.957 --> 00:04:33.997
The SaaS obligations are visible in a few places: a tenant on the token, a tenant predicate on every query,

33
00:04:33.997 --> 00:04:40.830
a control-plane record that can create and delete that tenant, a meter, and a stated silo-or-pool choice.

34
00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:45.252
An AI claim about a SaaS system has to show those.

35
00:04:45.252 --> 00:04:50.879
A system can satisfy the microservice claim and miss this one, or the reverse.

36
00:04:51.479 --> 00:04:54.645
Three things to carry away. One.

37
00:04:54.645 --> 00:05:04.669
SaaS means one product for many tenants — with a control plane that onboards, tiers, and meters them. Two.

38
00:05:04.669 --> 00:05:11.001
Tenant context rides on every request, message, log line, and query. Three.

39
00:05:11.001 --> 00:05:17.860
Microservices repeat the tenant obligations in every service; the platform cannot supply them.

40
00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:26.448
Next: there is no fourth ‘as a service’. Three models, sorted by who the customer is.
